# Buildroot defconfig for QEMU aarch64 virt + EDU driver development
#
# HOW TO USE:
#   cd buildroot
#   make BR2_DEFCONFIG=../buildroot-config/edu_defconfig defconfig
#   # (or manually apply the menuconfig changes listed below)
#   make -j$(sysctl -n hw.ncpu)
#
# Expected build time: 60-120 minutes on M4 Air (mostly downloading + compiling GCC).
# After first build, incremental rebuilds are fast.
#
# MANDATORY menuconfig changes (on top of qemu_aarch64_virt_defconfig):
#
# 1. Toolchain
#    → Kernel Headers: same version as the selected kernel (default is fine)
#    → [optional] Build cross gdb for host: Y (enables in-guest gdb later)
#
# 2. Target packages → Hardware handling
#    → pciutils: Y   (gives you `lspci` inside the guest)
#
# 3. System configuration → /dev management
#    → Dynamic using devtmpfs + eudev: Y
#       (ensures /dev/edu0 appears automatically when the module loads)
#
# 4. Kernel → Linux Kernel
#    → Defconfig name: virt    (this is already set by qemu_aarch64_virt_defconfig)
#    → Kernel version: choose a recent stable (6.1 LTS recommended)
#
# OUTPUT ARTIFACTS (used by boot_qemu.sh):
#   output/images/Image         — uncompressed aarch64 kernel image
#   output/images/rootfs.ext4   — root filesystem
#
# CROSS-COMPILER (for building kernel module from macOS):
#   output/host/bin/aarch64-buildroot-linux-gnu-gcc
#   KDIR = output/build/linux-*/
#
# SNAPSHOT: after configuring, run:
#   make savedefconfig DEFCONFIG=../buildroot-config/edu_defconfig
# to update this file with your exact configuration.

# This file will be overwritten by `make savedefconfig` with the actual
# Buildroot configuration syntax. The comments above are for human reference.
#
# Minimal saved config (placeholder until first `make savedefconfig`):
BR2_aarch64=y
BR2_TOOLCHAIN_BUILDROOT_GLIBC=y
BR2_LINUX_KERNEL=y
BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_DEFCONFIG="virt"
BR2_TARGET_ROOTFS_EXT2=y
BR2_TARGET_ROOTFS_EXT2_4=y
BR2_PACKAGE_PCIUTILS=y
BR2_ROOTFS_DEVICE_CREATION_DYNAMIC_EUDEV=y
